## S — Single Responsibility Principle

```java
// VIOLATION: OrderService does too much
public class OrderService {
    public void createOrder(Order order) {
        // Save to DB
        orderRepo.save(order);
        // Send email
        String body = "Your order #" + order.getId() + " is confirmed";
        emailSender.send(order.getUserEmail(), body);  // Email logic in OrderService!
        // Log
        logger.info("Order created: {}", order.getId());
    }
}

// CORRECT: Separate responsibilities
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final OrderEmailService emailService;  // Delegate emailing
    private final OrderAuditLogger auditLogger;    // Delegate logging

    public void createOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);
        emailService.sendOrderConfirmation(order);
        auditLogger.logCreated(order);
    }
}
```

## O — Open/Closed Principle

```java
// VIOLATION: Adding payment method requires modifying this class
public class PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(Payment payment) {
        if ("STRIPE".equals(payment.getMethod())) {
            stripeGateway.charge(payment);
        } else if ("PAYPAL".equals(payment.getMethod())) {  // ← Modification!
            paypalGateway.pay(payment);
        }
    }
}

// CORRECT: Extension without modification
public interface PaymentProvider {
    boolean supports(String method);
    void process(Payment payment);
}

@Component
public class StripePaymentProvider implements PaymentProvider { ... }

@Component
public class PayPalPaymentProvider implements PaymentProvider { ... }  // New — no change to processor

public class PaymentProcessor {
    private final List<PaymentProvider> providers;  // All discovered via DI

    public void process(Payment payment) {
        providers.stream()
            .filter(p -> p.supports(payment.getMethod()))
            .findFirst()
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UnsupportedPaymentMethodException(payment.getMethod()))
            .process(payment);
    }
}
```

## L — Liskov Substitution Principle

```java
// VIOLATION: Square extends Rectangle — breaks Liskov
public class Rectangle {
    protected int width, height;
    public void setWidth(int w) { this.width = w; }
    public void setHeight(int h) { this.height = h; }
    public int area() { return width * height; }
}

public class Square extends Rectangle {
    @Override
    public void setWidth(int w) { width = height = w; }  // Breaks contract!
    @Override
    public void setHeight(int h) { width = height = h; }
}

void resizeShape(Rectangle r) {
    r.setWidth(5);
    r.setHeight(10);
    assert r.area() == 50;  // Passes for Rectangle, FAILS for Square!
}
// Square cannot be substituted for Rectangle — Liskov violated

// CORRECT: Don't inherit where substitution breaks
public interface Shape { int area(); }
public class Rectangle implements Shape { ... }  // Separate hierarchies
public class Square implements Shape { ... }
```

## I — Interface Segregation Principle

```java
// VIOLATION: Fat interface forces classes to implement unused methods
public interface UserService {
    User findById(Long id);
    List<User> findAll();
    User createUser(CreateUserRequest req);
    void deleteUser(Long id);
    void resetPassword(Long id);
    List<AuditLog> getUserAuditLogs(Long id);  // Not all callers need this!
}

// CORRECT: Focused interfaces
public interface UserReader {
    User findById(Long id);
    List<User> findAll();
}

public interface UserWriter {
    User createUser(CreateUserRequest req);
    void deleteUser(Long id);
}

public interface UserAudit {
    List<AuditLog> getUserAuditLogs(Long id);
}

// OrderService only depends on what it needs:
public class OrderService {
    private final UserReader userReader;  // Not UserWriter, not UserAudit
}
```

## D — Dependency Inversion Principle

```java
// VIOLATION: High-level depends on low-level concrete
public class OrderService {
    private final StripePaymentGateway stripe = new StripePaymentGateway();  // Concrete!

    public void checkout(Order order) {
        stripe.charge(order.getAmount(), order.getCard());
    }
}
// Cannot swap payment provider without changing OrderService
// Cannot test without hitting Stripe

// CORRECT: Both depend on abstraction
public interface PaymentGateway {
    void charge(BigDecimal amount, Card card);
}

@Component
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;  // Interface!

    public OrderService(PaymentGateway paymentGateway) {
        this.paymentGateway = paymentGateway;  // Injected by Spring
    }

    public void checkout(Order order) {
        paymentGateway.charge(order.getAmount(), order.getCard());
    }
}

// In tests: inject MockPaymentGateway
// In prod: inject StripePaymentGateway
// OrderService never changes for payment reasons
```

## Production Reality

In interviews, connect SOLID to real problems: "I applied OCP when adding a new notification channel — instead of modifying the service, I added a new `NotificationChannel` implementation and registered it." Showing practical application is far more impressive than reciting definitions.

## Inheritance Problem — Fragile Base Class

```java
// Fragile base class problem:
public class MyHashSet<E> extends HashSet<E> {
    private int addCount = 0;

    @Override
    public boolean add(E e) {
        addCount++;           // Track additions
        return super.add(e);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c) {
        addCount += c.size(); // Count additions
        return super.addAll(c);
    }
}

MyHashSet<String> set = new MyHashSet<>();
set.addAll(List.of("a", "b", "c"));
System.out.println(set.addCount);  // Expected: 3, Actual: 6!

// Why? HashSet.addAll() internally calls add() for each element
// Our overridden add() gets called → counts each element again
// We're coupled to HashSet's internal implementation detail!
```

## Composition Solution

```java
// Composition: wrap, don't extend
public class InstrumentedSet<E> {
    private final Set<E> delegate;  // Composition!
    private int addCount = 0;

    public InstrumentedSet(Set<E> delegate) {
        this.delegate = delegate;
    }

    public boolean add(E e) {
        addCount++;
        return delegate.add(e);
    }

    public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c) {
        addCount += c.size();
        return delegate.addAll(c);  // Calls delegate's addAll, not our add() — correct!
    }

    // Forward all other Set methods to delegate
    public int size() { return delegate.size(); }
    public boolean contains(Object o) { return delegate.contains(o); }
    // ...
}
// Bonus: can wrap any Set implementation — TreeSet, LinkedHashSet, etc.
```

## Strategy Pattern — Swap Behavior at Runtime

```java
// Inheritance: behavior fixed at compile time
class TaxCalculator {
    protected BigDecimal calculateTax(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.1"));
    }
}
class UKTaxCalculator extends TaxCalculator { ... }  // Only one per instance

// Composition: swap behavior at runtime
public interface TaxStrategy {
    BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount);
}

@Component
public class OrderPricingService {
    private TaxStrategy taxStrategy;

    public void setTaxStrategy(TaxStrategy strategy) {
        this.taxStrategy = strategy;  // Can change based on user's country
    }

    public BigDecimal getTotal(Order order) {
        return order.getSubtotal().add(taxStrategy.calculate(order.getSubtotal()));
    }
}
```

## When Inheritance IS Appropriate

```java
// Genuine is-a relationships:
public abstract class Animal {
    abstract void makeSound();
    void breathe() { /* shared behavior */ }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() { System.out.println("Woof"); }
    // Dog IS-A Animal — correct use
}

// Exception hierarchies:
public class OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException { ... }  // IS-A
public class PaymentException extends BusinessException { ... }  // IS-A

// Abstract template method pattern:
public abstract class ReportGenerator {
    // Template method — defines algorithm structure
    public final Report generate(ReportRequest req) {
        List<Data> data = fetchData(req);      // Subclass provides
        List<Row> rows = processData(data);    // Common
        return formatReport(rows);              // Subclass provides
    }
    protected abstract List<Data> fetchData(ReportRequest req);
    protected abstract Report formatReport(List<Row> rows);
}
```

## Production Reality

Composition makes code testable — you can inject mocks for each collaborating object. Inheritance forces you to use the real superclass or create test-specific subclasses. In Spring applications, almost everything is composition via dependency injection. Use inheritance for genuine type hierarchies (exceptions, entity hierarchies, abstract base classes with shared behavior), not for code reuse.

## Without Polymorphism — Conditional Explosion

```java
// Every new notification type requires modifying this method:
public void sendNotification(Notification notification) {
    if (notification.getType() == NotificationType.EMAIL) {
        emailService.send(notification.getRecipient(), notification.getBody());
    } else if (notification.getType() == NotificationType.SMS) {
        smsService.send(notification.getPhone(), notification.getBody());
    } else if (notification.getType() == NotificationType.PUSH) {   // New type added
        pushService.push(notification.getDeviceToken(), notification.getBody());
    } else if (notification.getType() == NotificationType.SLACK) {  // Another new type
        slackService.post(notification.getChannel(), notification.getBody());
    }
    // This grows forever — Open/Closed violation
}
```

## With Polymorphism — Open for Extension

```java
public interface NotificationSender {
    NotificationType supports();
    void send(Notification notification);
}

@Component
public class EmailNotificationSender implements NotificationSender {
    @Override
    public NotificationType supports() { return NotificationType.EMAIL; }
    @Override
    public void send(Notification n) { emailService.send(n.getRecipient(), n.getBody()); }
}

@Component
public class SmsNotificationSender implements NotificationSender {
    @Override
    public NotificationType supports() { return NotificationType.SMS; }
    @Override
    public void send(Notification n) { smsService.send(n.getPhone(), n.getBody()); }
}

// NotificationService — never changes when adding new types:
@Service
public class NotificationService {
    private final Map<NotificationType, NotificationSender> senders;

    public NotificationService(List<NotificationSender> senderList) {
        // Spring injects all NotificationSender beans
        senders = senderList.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                NotificationSender::supports,
                Function.identity()
            ));
    }

    public void send(Notification notification) {
        NotificationSender sender = senders.get(notification.getType());
        if (sender == null) throw new UnsupportedNotificationTypeException(notification.getType());
        sender.send(notification);  // Polymorphic dispatch!
    }
}
// Add Slack sender: create SlackNotificationSender bean — nothing else changes
```

## instanceof as a Code Smell

```java
// SMELL: instanceof checking
public double calculateArea(Shape shape) {
    if (shape instanceof Circle c) {
        return Math.PI * c.getRadius() * c.getRadius();
    } else if (shape instanceof Rectangle r) {
        return r.getWidth() * r.getHeight();
    }
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown shape");
}

// CORRECT: Polymorphism
public interface Shape {
    double area();  // Each shape knows its own area
}

public class Circle implements Shape {
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

public class Rectangle implements Shape {
    public double area() { return width * height; }
}

// Calling code:
double area = shape.area();  // No instanceof, no conditionals
```

## Production Reality

When you find yourself writing `if (type == X || type == Y)` repeatedly, that's a polymorphism opportunity. Map the types to their behaviors at construction time (registry pattern) and dispatch polymorphically. This pattern — collect implementations via DI, dispatch by type — appears in Spring itself: `HandlerMapping`, `MessageConverter`, `AuthenticationProvider` all use it.

## Interface — Capability Contract

```java
// Multiple capabilities per class:
public interface Serializable { ... }  // Can be serialized
public interface Comparable<T> { int compareTo(T other); }  // Can be compared
public interface Cloneable { ... }  // Can be cloned

public class Order implements Serializable, Comparable<Order> {
    // Implements multiple capabilities — not possible with abstract class

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Order other) {
        return this.createdAt.compareTo(other.createdAt);
    }
}

// Default methods (Java 8+): provide default implementation
public interface Validator<T> {
    boolean isValid(T value);

    // Default method — optional to override
    default void validate(T value) {
        if (!isValid(value)) {
            throw new ValidationException("Invalid: " + value);
        }
    }

    // Static method — factory/utility in the interface
    static <T> Validator<T> notNull() {
        return value -> value != null;
    }
}
```

## Abstract Class — Shared Base with State

```java
// When you need: shared state, constructors, protected methods
public abstract class BaseRepository<T, ID> {
    // Shared state — not possible in interface
    protected final EntityManager em;
    protected final Class<T> entityClass;

    // Constructor required to initialize state
    protected BaseRepository(EntityManager em, Class<T> entityClass) {
        this.em = em;
        this.entityClass = entityClass;
    }

    // Shared implementation
    public Optional<T> findById(ID id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(em.find(entityClass, id));
    }

    public T save(T entity) {
        return em.merge(entity);
    }

    // Abstract: subclass must provide
    protected abstract String buildFindAllQuery();

    // Protected template: subclass can call but not override access
    protected List<T> findAll() {
        return em.createQuery(buildFindAllQuery(), entityClass).getResultList();
    }
}

// Subclass only adds what's unique:
public class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<Order, Long> {
    public OrderRepository(EntityManager em) {
        super(em, Order.class);
    }

    @Override
    protected String buildFindAllQuery() {
        return "SELECT o FROM Order o ORDER BY o.createdAt DESC";
    }

    // Add Order-specific queries:
    public List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status) { ... }
}
```

## Skeletal Implementation Pattern (Best of Both)

```java
// Interface: defines the contract
public interface NotificationSender {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
    boolean isAvailable();
    int getMaxRetries();
    Duration getRetryDelay();
}

// Abstract skeletal implementation: provides defaults for optional behavior
public abstract class AbstractNotificationSender implements NotificationSender {
    @Override
    public int getMaxRetries() { return 3; }  // Default: subclass can override

    @Override
    public Duration getRetryDelay() { return Duration.ofSeconds(2); }  // Default

    // Template method with retry logic:
    public void sendWithRetry(String recipient, String message) {
        for (int i = 0; i < getMaxRetries(); i++) {
            try {
                send(recipient, message);
                return;
            } catch (NotificationException e) {
                if (i == getMaxRetries() - 1) throw e;
                sleepFor(getRetryDelay());
            }
        }
    }
}

// Concrete implementation only focuses on the unique part:
public class EmailSender extends AbstractNotificationSender {
    @Override
    public void send(String recipient, String message) {
        smtpClient.send(recipient, message);  // Just the email logic
    }
    @Override
    public boolean isAvailable() { return smtpClient.isConnected(); }
    // Inherits: getMaxRetries(), getRetryDelay(), sendWithRetry()
}
```

## Production Reality

In Spring applications: use interfaces for service contracts (allows mocking in tests and multiple implementations). Use abstract classes for template-method patterns where the base class defines the algorithm and subclasses fill in the steps. Spring itself follows this pattern extensively: `AbstractController`, `AbstractJpaRepository`, `AbstractMessageConverter`.

## Strategy Pattern (Most Common in Spring)

```java
// Strategy interface:
public interface PricingStrategy {
    BigDecimal calculatePrice(Product product, User user);
}

// Strategies:
@Component
public class StandardPricingStrategy implements PricingStrategy {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculatePrice(Product p, User u) { return p.getBasePrice(); }
}

@Component
public class MemberPricingStrategy implements PricingStrategy {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculatePrice(Product p, User u) {
        return p.getBasePrice().multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(0.9));  // 10% off
    }
}

// Context — uses the right strategy:
@Service
public class PricingService {
    @Autowired
    private List<PricingStrategy> strategies;  // All implementations injected

    public BigDecimal getPrice(Product product, User user) {
        return selectStrategy(user).calculatePrice(product, user);
    }

    private PricingStrategy selectStrategy(User user) {
        return user.isMember() ? memberStrategy : standardStrategy;
    }
}
```

## Builder Pattern

```java
// Builder for complex objects:
public class Order {
    private final Long userId;
    private final List<OrderItem> items;
    private final ShippingAddress address;
    private final PaymentMethod payment;
    private final String promoCode;

    private Order(Builder builder) {
        this.userId = Objects.requireNonNull(builder.userId);
        this.items = Objects.requireNonNull(builder.items);
        this.address = Objects.requireNonNull(builder.address);
        this.payment = Objects.requireNonNull(builder.payment);
        this.promoCode = builder.promoCode;  // Optional
    }

    public static Builder builder() { return new Builder(); }

    public static class Builder {
        private Long userId;
        private List<OrderItem> items;
        private ShippingAddress address;
        private PaymentMethod payment;
        private String promoCode;

        public Builder userId(Long id) { this.userId = id; return this; }
        public Builder items(List<OrderItem> items) { this.items = items; return this; }
        public Builder address(ShippingAddress addr) { this.address = addr; return this; }
        public Builder payment(PaymentMethod pm) { this.payment = pm; return this; }
        public Builder promoCode(String code) { this.promoCode = code; return this; }
        public Order build() { return new Order(this); }
    }
}

// Usage:
Order order = Order.builder()
    .userId(42L)
    .items(items)
    .address(deliveryAddress)
    .payment(card)
    .promoCode("SAVE10")
    .build();

// Lombok simplifies:
@Builder
@Value  // Immutable
public class Order { ... }
```

## Observer via Spring Events

```java
// Event:
public record OrderCreatedEvent(Order order, User user) {}

// Publisher:
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private ApplicationEventPublisher eventPublisher;

    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) {
        Order order = orderRepo.save(new Order(req));
        eventPublisher.publishEvent(new OrderCreatedEvent(order, req.getUser()));
        // Decoupled — OrderService doesn't know who listens
        return order;
    }
}

// Listeners — add without touching OrderService:
@Component
public class OrderEmailListener {
    @EventListener
    @Async  // Don't block the main transaction
    public void onOrderCreated(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
        emailService.sendOrderConfirmation(event.order(), event.user());
    }
}

@Component
public class InventoryUpdateListener {
    @EventListener
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)  // Only if tx committed
    public void onOrderCreated(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
        inventoryService.decrementStock(event.order().getItems());
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

`@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` is critically important for listeners that do follow-up work — like sending emails or calling external APIs. If the transaction rolls back, you don't want the email sent. Using `AFTER_COMMIT` ensures the listener only runs when the transaction successfully commits.

## Java Record Basics

```java
// Record — one line for an immutable data class:
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {}

// Equivalent to:
public final class Money {
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final Currency currency;

    public Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {
        this.amount = amount;
        this.currency = currency;
    }
    public BigDecimal amount() { return amount; }
    public Currency currency() { return currency; }
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { ... }  // By components
    @Override public int hashCode() { ... }  // By components
    @Override public String toString() { ... }  // By components
}
```

## Records with Custom Validation

```java
// Compact constructor for validation:
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {

    // Compact constructor (no parameter list — uses record components)
    public Money {
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount, "amount must not be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, "currency must not be null");
        if (amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("amount must be non-negative");
        }
        amount = amount.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);  // Normalize
    }

    // Custom methods:
    public Money add(Money other) {
        if (!currency.equals(other.currency)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot add different currencies");
        }
        return new Money(amount.add(other.amount), currency);
    }

    public boolean isZero() { return amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0; }
}
```

## Records for DTOs and Events

```java
// API request DTO:
public record CreateOrderRequest(
    @NotNull Long userId,
    @NotEmpty List<OrderItemRequest> items,
    @NotNull ShippingAddressRequest address
) {}

// API response DTO:
public record OrderResponse(
    Long id,
    String status,
    BigDecimal total,
    LocalDateTime createdAt
) {
    // Factory method from entity:
    public static OrderResponse from(Order order) {
        return new OrderResponse(
            order.getId(),
            order.getStatus().name(),
            order.getTotal(),
            order.getCreatedAt()
        );
    }
}

// Spring Event:
public record OrderShippedEvent(Long orderId, String trackingNumber, Instant shippedAt) {}

// JPQL DTO projection:
@Query("SELECT new com.example.dto.UserOrderCount(u.id, u.name, COUNT(o)) "
     + "FROM Order o JOIN o.user u GROUP BY u.id, u.name")
List<UserOrderCount> getUserOrderCounts();

public record UserOrderCount(Long userId, String userName, Long orderCount) {}
```

## Immutability for Thread Safety

```java
// Mutable — needs synchronization:
public class Counter {
    private int count = 0;  // Shared mutable state
    public synchronized int increment() { return ++count; }  // Synchronized
    public synchronized int getCount() { return count; }
}

// Immutable — no synchronization needed:
public record ImmutableCounter(int count) {
    public ImmutableCounter increment() {
        return new ImmutableCounter(count + 1);  // Returns new instance
    }
}
// Safe to share across threads — no mutation possible

// Defensive copy for mutable fields:
public record OrderSnapshot(LocalDateTime timestamp, List<OrderItem> items) {
    public OrderSnapshot {
        timestamp = Objects.requireNonNull(timestamp);
        items = List.copyOf(items);  // Defensive copy — caller can't modify our list
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Use records for all DTOs, request/response objects, domain events, and value objects. Replace Lombok `@Value` with records where possible — records are language-native, no annotation processing needed, and show up clearly in the IDE. The one limitation: records can't extend classes, so they don't work as JPA entities or for inheritance hierarchies.
